welcome to the daily climate show coming  up as the uk looks set for another heat  wave who is at risk and to what extent  is climate change affecting our summer  weather  as senior conservatives battle it out  for the leadership the government's  chief scientific advisor briefs mps on  the risks of climate change  and squirrel contraception as a climate  solution yes you heard that right  [Music]  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show where we track the changes  happening to our world hear from the  people hit hardest by those changes and  report on the race for solutions now  it's hot and getting hotter not just the  forecast for this week of course but for  the rest of the century as the world  burns more fossil fuels the science is  unequivocal and the data is clear the  uk's 10 warmest years on record have all  occurred since 2002.  we'll look at that context shortly but  first we can talk to aisha zahid who is  in richmond in south west london for us  aisha the met office today issuing a  risk to life warning how are people  coping with the heat where you are  well people have been enjoying the  sunshine but also taking precautions of  course this  weather can have adverse effects on  health and be that dehydration heat  stroke heat exhaustion it can also  exacerbate symptoms for people with  underlying conditions like heart and  lung problems as well and heat heat  waves can affect  virtually anyone but there are  particular groups more at risk than  others and that includes elderly people  people who live alone babies and  children people who work outdoors and  like i mentioned people who do have  underlying health conditions as well so  the uk health security agency is really  urging people to be cautious to make  sure they stay hydrated to make sure  they're using sunscreen and try and  avoid going outdoors between 11 in the  morning and 3 o'clock in the afternoon  or trying to avoid direct sunlight at  least because that's when uv rays are at  their strongest  hi sure in richmond in south west london  thanks so much for that update well  let's look at the context here i'm  joined by our science correspondent  thomas moore  thomas we just talked about how the uk's  10 warmest years on record have all  occurred since 2002. this is much more  than just a bit of summery weather isn't  it very much so and if you look at the  the summer temperatures too they're  getting progressively warmer if you look  all the way back more than a century the  five warmest summers  have all been since  1976 and if you look forward and there  is increasing concern about what  happened this century so the met office  has done some analysis of the year 2018  which is a very warm summer the warmest  on on record uh jointly with other years  but they found that a warm summer  of that temperature was 30 times more  likely as a result of all the greenhouse  gases in the atmosphere and if you look  at specific temperatures they believe  that  by mid-century the chances of having a  warm summer  are going to be 50-50 so you'll have a  really warm summer every other year and  by the end of the century the chances of  temperatures going over 40 degrees at  some point in a year will be  once every three to four years it really  is going to be very warm indeed just  really quickly while we've got you this  is really serious people are dying from  climate change yeah very much so if you  look at recent years there's been an  average of 2 000 deaths a year  from heat going forward  the predictions are that that will  increase to 7 000 a year so this isn't  just uncomfortable it really is deadly  thomas more our science correspondent  thank you so much for that well there'll  be plenty of coverage this week about  the heat and the context behind it  together with advice on how to keep cool  all on our website skynews.com  well elsewhere  portugal is also in the midst of a  heatwave with temperatures expected to  reach over 40 degrees tomorrow wildfires  have broken out across northern and  central parts of the country with about  3 000 firefighters battling to control  the flames portugal has already seen  extreme weather this year with a drought  affecting around a quarter of the  country last month  and over in the united states yosemite  national park in california has been hit  by wildfires threatening the largest  grove of sequoia trees the trees are  known for their size and age with one  thought to be more than three thousand  years old well let's get a bit more of  today's climate news now the biggest  pipeline carrying russian gas to germany  has begun annual maintenance work with  flows expected to stop for 10 days but  germany is bracing itself for the  possibility of russia imposing a longer  or even permanent shutdown of the nord  stream pipeline due to tensions over the  war in ukraine the german parliament  recently approved the reactivation of  coal plants following a reduction in gas  supply from russia  well this comes as the eu's climate  chief warned europe is at risk of civil  unrest this winter over high energy  prices franz timmerman said climate  goals would not be met if society  descends into very strong conflict and  strife speaking to the guardian he said  countries should make a short-term  return to fossil fuels to ease tensions  and keep industry functioning  a new birth control plan to reduce the  number of grey squirrels in the uk is  showing promising signs say researchers  the government-backed scheme involves  using a feeder that excludes most other  wildlife to give squirrels an oral  contraception grey squirrels damage  woodlands by stripping bark from young  trees something which could hit efforts  to fight climate change as planting more  trees is a key strategy to reducing  carbon in the atmosphere  well shortly after boris johnson became  prime minister he received a briefing  from the government's chief scientific  advisor which he credits with changing  his mind about climate change today more  mps are receiving a briefing from sir  patrick valance following a campaign  from angus rose who went on hunger  strike outside parliament he only ended  his strike after 37 days without food  when mps were offered the briefing  the uk is currently  aiming to have a vastly disproportionate  share of the globally limited carbon  budget  so i did this hunger strike out of  desperation  to try and secure a livable future for  my five nephews and  niece  um  a good way to understand it i think is  if i was living in ukraine as ukrainian  i'd be risking my life  to defend to protect my family and  community and those people who care  about me well joining me now is the  former editor of the british medical  journal and one of those who has been  pushing for this briefing that's dr  fiona godley fenia thank you so much for  joining us today it seems crazy to me  that this is the first ever high-level  briefing to mps on climate change  it is extraordinary and i agree um i i  suppose we just have to say it's  brilliant that it's happening  and it's a real opportunity for mps from  all parties to hear an official update  on climate science and to understand the  urgency of action and to explore you  know what is happening and what needs to  happen uh because as you've heard you  know earlier on in your show the urgency  is real  um we're talking about hitting the 1.5  degree tipping point into catastrophic  climate change possibly sometime around  2033 on latest estimates that's in 11  years time it's really inconceivable to  think that that is the case  and we've got a very strong consensus  now among scientists and health  professionals that that tipping point  will lead to really catastrophic  consequences for human health and  survival  and what can sir patrick valance tell  mps that they don't already know i mean  there's so much information available  well that's absolutely right and i think  that's one reason why some may think  they need not attend but i think what's  really important one of the things i've  been thinking about is where is the  kovid style response to climate change  you know we had daily updates from sir  patrick rightly so and to chris whitty  um and and others about covid um and  that's the kind of more than that the  level we need in relation to climate  change because the consequences of  catastrophic climate change is so much  greater  around the world but also in this  country so um you know what can he tell  them he needs to give them an update  they need to understand the urgency they  need to see this as a crucial priority  for all politicians uh to lead us  through this very difficult time  and uh we've obviously got the tory  leadership race at the moment what do  you make of what you've been hearing  from those hopefuls and what do you want  to be hearing from them  well i think we have to see this as  beyond party politics of course but we  are at a very interesting moment when a  small number of the general public will  be in a position to vote our next prime  minister into office and i very much  hope that those people will take climate  change as one of their key priorities  we'll listen to the candidates messages  on this we'll understand that the  climate change committee says we're  failing on all but eight we're only um  we're only ahead on eight of the i think  50 or so key indicators for um reducing  our climate burden and those ones we're  failing on our transport  energy efficiency um housing and  public engagement with the whole issue  around climate change so there's an  enormous job to be done if we are really  to to to to prevent what i think you  know will be within our lifetime and  certainly within the lifetimes of our  children and grandchildren  a very much very much more dangerous  place to live and possibly  uh you know unsurvivable temperatures  and very quickly well we've got you do  you think these sorts of meetings these  briefings for mps should become a  regular thing  well i would like to see that and i  think that's something we should applaud  you know the fact that this is happening  i think we would like to see it  happening on a regular basis and a lot  of the tracking that could be done um  you know how are we doing on on reducing  our carbon emissions carbon dioxide  stays in the atmosphere for centuries so  it's not just a question of you know how  we're doing we've got to look at our  historic  carbon emissions as well so i think it  would be a really good thing for our  political leaders to to  to to to be really encouraged by their  constituents uh to to engage with this  and to report back to their constituents  about what they're hearing and what  they're doing about it themselves and as  a par as as within their political  parties  dr fiona godley former editor of the  british medical journal thank you very  much for joining us  thank you and just before we leave you  this is the moment british climber harry  shimon feared for his life as an  avalanche came thundering down a  mountain towards him in kyrgyzstan  global warming may make avalanches like  this much more likely as warming  temperatures destabilise mountain  climates melt glaciers and create more  intense storms that is all from us today  thank you so much for watching we'll see  you next time

